  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Both ACE inhibitors and ARBs (Angiotensin Receptor Blockers) are recommended as equal class-I first-line treatments for high blood pressure, though are increasingly favored due to a lower risk of side effects.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "Both ACE inhibitors and ARBs (Angiotensin Receptor Blockers) are recommended as equal class-I first-line treatments for high blood pressure"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "How They Work"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "ACE inhibitors (ending in -pril, such as lisinopril and enalapril) block the body from producing angiotensin II, a chemical that narrows blood vessels.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              3,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "ARBs (ending in -sartan, such as losartan and valsartan) block angiotensin II receptors directly, keeping the chemical from binding and tightening blood vessels."
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          2,
          3,
          4
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Efficacy (Effectiveness)"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Blood Pressure & Outcomes: Large-scale studies, including the analysis of nearly 3 million patients, show that both drug classes are equally effective at lowering blood pressure and preventing major cardiovascular events like heart attacks and strokes."
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          1,
          5
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Side Effects and Tolerability"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "ACE inhibitors have a higher rate of side effects, most notably a persistent dry cough (affecting roughly 20% of users) and a rare, potentially serious swelling of the face or tongue known as angioedema (which occurs more frequently in African American patients).",
            "reference_indexes": [
              5,
              6,
              7
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "ARBs provide a much better safety and tolerability profile with significantly lower rates of discontinuation, cough, and .",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              8
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Shared risks: Both classes can raise blood potassium levels (hyperkalemia) and must be combined with each other or taken during pregnancy."
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          2,
          3,
          7
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Read more about drug guidelines via the American Heart Association through the AHA Journals Hypertension Report or compare specific properties via GoodRx ACE Inhibitors vs. ARBs Guide .",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "text": "GoodRx ACE Inhibitors vs. ARBs Guide",
            "link": "https://www.goodrx.com/conditions/hypertension/ace-inhibitors-vs-arbs-whats-the-difference"
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          2
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Are you looking at starting a specific medication, or do you have a history of side effects like a cough with blood pressure pills?"
      }
    ],
